# The key agent use case

Imagine an agent has:

```json
{
  "task": "large coding task",
  "expected_requests": 240,
  "expected_cached_tokens_per_request": 55000,
  "budget_usd": 4.00,
  "available_routes": [
    "opencode-go",
    "zai",
    "openrouter"
  ]
}
```

Without LiveLLM it has to:

```text
search OpenCode docs
search Z.ai docs
search OpenRouter
identify current promotions
distinguish list/current prices
understand cache pricing
find usage limits
check expiration dates
normalize all units
avoid stale search snippets
do arithmetic
```

And doing this with an LLM is exactly where you get stale knowledge, hallucinated prices and misunderstood promotions.

Instead:

```http
POST /v1/economics/route
```

and receive one compact answer.

Something like:

```json
{
  "as_of": "2026-08-30T02:50:00Z",
  "max_age_seconds": 21600,

  "workload": {
    "uncached_input_tokens": 1000,
    "cached_input_tokens": 55000,
    "output_tokens": 200,
    "requests": 240
  },

  "recommendation": {
    "route": "zai:glm-5.3-flash",
    "estimated_cost_usd": 0.234,
    "reason": "Current 50% PAYG promotion"
  },

  "alternatives": [
    {
      "route": "opencode-go:glm-5.3-flash",
      "plan_type": "subscription",
      "monthly_price_usd": 10,
      "promotion": {
        "type": "usage_multiplier",
        "multiplier": 2,
        "expires_at": null
      }
    }
  ],

  "facts": {
    "zai_input_usd_per_million": 0.075,
    "zai_cached_input_usd_per_million": 0.015,
    "zai_output_usd_per_million": 0.25
  },

  "confidence": 0.98,

  "provenance": {
    "facts_verified": 7,
    "official_sources": 2,
    "serpapi_search_ids": [
      "..."
    ]
  }
}
```

The **absence** of an expiry date for OpenCode is meaningful:

```json
"expires_at": null
```

rather than inventing one.

That is the product philosophy.

