# And then x402 makes considerable sense

This is one of the rare products where x402 isn't just hackathon crypto garnish.

An autonomous agent probably doesn't want:

```text
create account
choose subscription
enter credit card
obtain API key
manage another SaaS bill
```

to answer a $0.002 question.

x402 is specifically designed to let a client hit a protected resource, receive a `402 Payment Required`, make/authorize the payment and retry the request. Current x402 V2 explicitly supports agent-oriented resource payments and separates the payment logic from transports such as HTTP, MCP and A2A. ([GitHub][2])

So:

```text
Agent
  │
  │ GET /v1/market
  ▼
LiveLLM
  │
  └─ 402 Payment Required
            │
            ▼
       agent pays
            │
            ▼
       retry request
            │
            ▼
   verified market payload
```

That's coherent.

And x402's own current MCP guidance demonstrates the same pattern: an MCP-compatible agent encounters the paid API, handles the 402/payment handshake automatically, and receives the resource. ([GitHub][3])

